cutlass_extensions/ -- TensorRT-LLM's CUTLASS epilogue extensions
=================================================================

Provenance
----------

Upstream is NOT CUTLASS.  These headers are NVIDIA's TensorRT-LLM additions
to CUTLASS, carried inside FlashInfer's source tree at

    csrc/nv_internal/tensorrt_llm/cutlass_extensions/include/cutlass_extensions/

and fetched by the `CPMAddPackage` at `crates/kernels-cuda/csrc/CMakeLists.txt`
while that file still exists.  The distinction matters: a patch to a CUTLASS
header is a patch to a widely-used upstream and wants to go there; a patch to
`cutlass_extensions` is a patch to a vendored fork of a vendored fork, and
nobody upstream is waiting for it.

The directory is spelled `cutlass_extensions/...` rather than
`tensorrt_llm/cutlass_extensions/...` because that is the spelling its
includers write -- `moe_gemm_tma_ws_launcher.inl` says

    #include "cutlass_extensions/epilogue/fusion/sm90_visitor_scatter.hpp"

and the carried set is rooted so that the name an includer writes is the name
the set carries.

Why only one file
-----------------

Because a compile should not carry what it cannot need, and the closure has
not been computed yet.  The three candidate trees measure 710 files / 16 MB
(cutlass), 112 / 12 MB (cute) and 8,172 / 67 MB (cccl); the carried set is
`include_str!`, so carrying them whole is binary size, and the CUB closure was
already refused once at 429 files / 4.2 MB for four kernels doing an exclusive
sum of an `int`.

This file is here ahead of its closure for one specific reason: **it is
patched, and the patch is invisible to whoever computes the closure later.**
A closure computation copies files out of the CPM tree.  It would copy the
unpatched `sm90_visitor_scatter.hpp` and silently reintroduce a compile
failure that took a full probe cycle to diagnose.  A patched file sitting
here with its `// PIE:` markers is what stops that.

Relationship to ../MODIFICATIONS
--------------------------------

`csrc/vendor/MODIFICATIONS` and the three tests in
`tests/vendor_manifest.rs` that keep it honest are scoped to
`csrc/vendor/flashinfer/` -- `vendored()` roots its walk there, so the file
table, the byte counts and the `// PIE:` marker total all describe that
subtree and only that subtree.  `csrc/vendor/xqa/` already sits outside the
same scope.

That is a scope gap, not a licence.  The property MODIFICATIONS asserts --
*strip every `// PIE:` marker and its guard and you get upstream back, byte
for byte* -- is the property this file holds too, and the transform for it is
purely textual:

    sm90_visitor_scatter.hpp:  2 markers, 2 changed lines
      `size<1>(decltype(args.epi_tile){})`  ->  `size<1>(args.epi_tile)`
      `size<0>(decltype(args.epi_tile){})`  ->  `size<0>(args.epi_tile)`

When the CUTLASS closure lands, this subtree should come under
MODIFICATIONS' table rather than keep its own note, and this file should be
deleted in the same change.  It is written down here in the meantime because
an undocumented patch is how vendoring becomes a fork.

What the patch buys
-------------------

Measured under `libnvrtc.so.13`, `--gpu-architecture=compute_90a`,
`-std=c++17 -default-device`: with it, the sm90 pointer-array grouped GEMM
carrying the FINALIZE scatter epilogue -- the whole `GemmUniversal` device
body, not a fragment -- compiles rc=0 to 3,500,029 bytes of PTX with exactly
one `.entry`, and `nvrtcGetLoweredName` returns its mangled name.  Without
it, `expression must have a constant value` at
`sm90_visitor_scatter.hpp:301`, followed by a cascade of
`cute::C<<error-constant>>` incomplete-type errors from `cute/container/
tuple.hpp` as the poisoned constant propagates through the layout algebra.

This is the finding that retires the `_UNRESOLVED` on the FINALIZE epilogue
recorded in `.wiki/driver/new-horizon.md` §62.7.  The earlier refusal was
diagnosed at the time as "my parameterisation, not NVRTC", and that diagnosis
was half right: the parameterisation was indeed wrong (see the corrected
`using` chain in `crates/driver-cuda/src/fire/flashinfer_moe.rs`), but fixing
it exposed this second, real, frontend-conformance issue underneath.  Two
faults in series is why the first probe read as a wall.
